WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.980
So GDP is down, retail sales have fallen off
a cliff, and unemployment is the worst it’s

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:10.589
ever been, but the stock market… is doing
pretty good?

00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:11.589
What gives?

00:00:11.589 --> 00:00:15.209
Have all these traders been living in a cave
for the last three months?

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:20.220
Or maybe they’re ahead of the curve, and
they already know that the economy is on the

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.220
mend?

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:25.390
That’s a nice thought, but it’s probably
safer to stick to Paul Krugman’s three rules

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:30.740
about interpreting stock prices: “First,
the stock market is not the economy.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.270
Second, the stock market is not the economy.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.480
Third, the stock market is not the economy.”

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.920
A lot of the rosy numbers are being driven
by big tech companies like Amazon and Apple,

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.090
who are doing gangbusters with everyone trapped
at home.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:48.840
So-called “early cycle” stocks, which
tend to be more reflective of the real economy,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.969
like cars, banks and consumer goods, are lagging
behind.

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:57.050
And thousands of small businesses crippled
by the pandemic are not represented in the

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.550
stock market at all.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:04.000
Some economists describe what’s happening
as a market “de-coupling,” when stock

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.640
prices cease to reflect economic realities.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:13.040
Most forecasters now predict a lot of bad
numbers in the coming months, yet investors

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.540
are strangely optimistic.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:20.320
The warning lights are all flashing red, but
the market seems to be blissfully unaware

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.910
of the storm on the horizon.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:23.060
If you’re thinking, “Wait a minute.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:27.970
I thought stock traders were supposed to be
really logical people,” ...well, you just

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:33.160
waded into a century-long debate about the
very nature of the stock market.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.790
Is it a rational mechanism that accurately
reflects what companies are worth?

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:44.000
Or is it governed by the touchy-feely emotions
and biases that all humans are subject to?

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:58.380
There’s a long tradition of using emotional
phrases to describe the world of finance.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.900
The Great Depression.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.660
The Panic of 1837.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.570
Tulip Mania.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.739
Investors are often described as “optimistic”
and “cheery,” or “skittish” and “glum.”

00:02:08.739 --> 00:02:13.310
Alan Greenspan famously warned about traders’
“irrational exuberance” during the dot

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:18.549
com bubble, and there’s an old Wall Street
saying that “financial markets are driven

00:02:18.549 --> 00:02:22.400
by two powerful emotions: greed and fear.”

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.819
And yet, for much of the 20th century, the
consensus among economists was that the market

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:31.769
was perfectly rational and stock prices were
always an accurate reflection of a company’s

00:02:31.769 --> 00:02:33.010
true value.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:38.269
According to the “efficient market hypothesis,”
investors react to good or bad news about

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:43.409
a company, driving the price up or down until
the risk is balanced with the reward.

00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:48.400
Therefore, in theory, a company can never
be “undervalued” or “overvalued.”

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.209
Since all public information about a company
is already reflected in its stock price, it’s

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:55.249
impossible to “beat the market.”

00:02:55.249 --> 00:03:00.900
Many years earlier, John Maynard Keynes, one
of the godfathers of modern economics, doubted

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.800
that a dispersed pool of investors could really
know a company’s true value.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.930
He compared the stock market to an offensively
outdated little contest the British newspapers

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.430
would sometimes hold in the 1930s.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:19.519
They would print the photos of a hundred women
and ask readers to pick the six prettiest

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:20.519
faces.

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:25.639
The winner would be the person whose choices
most closely matched the average choices of

00:03:25.639 --> 00:03:26.790
all the contestants.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:31.519
The problem with this (aside from it being
an ancestor to Hot or Not) is that shrewd

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:36.430
contestants would not pick the faces they
actually thought were the prettiest, or even

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.939
the faces that they thought the average person
would find the prettiest, but the faces that

00:03:40.939 --> 00:03:46.349
they thought the average person would guess
the average person would find the prettiest.

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:50.859
If you think your head is spinning now, consider
another version of this dilemma called the

00:03:50.859 --> 00:03:52.310
“2/3rds Game.”

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:56.180
A bunch of contestants are asked to pick a
number between 1 and 100.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:01.700
The winning number is the one that is closest
to 2/3rds of the average of all the numbers.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.409
So if the average is 50, whoever picks 33
wins.

00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:11.340
But, assuming most of the contestants can
do basic math, they’ll probably choose 33,

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.390
which means the winning number is now 22.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:18.530
And if most of the contestants anticipate
this, they’ll likely choose 22, making the

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:19.530
winning number 15.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:24.090
And if they all guess that the others are
thinking the same way, they’ll choose 15

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.020
and the winning number is now 10.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:30.070
Depending on how clever the group is, the
winning number can quickly dwindle down to

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.070
zero.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:35.480
These examples are meant to illustrate that
when people buy and sell stocks, they’re

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:40.340
not always trying to predict something as
concrete as profitability.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.950
Many speculative traders are simply trying
to guess what other people will be willing

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:50.700
to spend on a stock in the future, which is
dependent on what those people think other

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.160
people will be willing to spend.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:05:00.310
Much like the 2/3rds game, this can cause
a cascading effect of over- or undervaluation.

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:05.320
In the 1980s, the Nobel Prize-winning economist
Richard Thaler set out to prove that it was

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:11.600
possible for companies to be over- or undervalued
by building two theoretical portfolios of

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.380
stocks: “Winners” and “Losers.”

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.710
The “Winners” portfolio was made up of
companies whose stock price had recently performed

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:24.330
exceptionally well, and the “Losers” portfolio
was full of stocks that had recently performed

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.370
exceptionally poorly.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:31.580
His theory was that these movements were fueled
by investors being over-enthusiastic about

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.710
the winners and overly-pessimistic about the
losers.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:40.860
If he was right, then all these extreme prices
would regress back to the mean, causing the

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.300
“Losers” portfolio to perform better.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:45.940
And that’s exactly what happened.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:53.420
In survey after survey, the Losers performed
better than the Winners, often by a wide margin.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:58.980
It seemed to suggest that stock prices can
be influenced by what Keynes called investors’

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:04.530
“animal spirits,” or what psychologists
might call “herd behavior”--jumping on

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.750
and off bandwagons because everyone else is.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:13.610
What Thaler had tested was a form of “value
investing”--betting that certain companies

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:16.610
are being undervalued by the market.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:22.640
The efficient market hypothesis said this
should be impossible, but tell that to Warren

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:23.880
Buffet.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:27.470
And then, something happened that Thaler believed
settled the debate.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:28.470
Good evening.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:30.210
Today is Black Monday.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:33.550
The day the DOW dropped more than 500 points.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:34.880
Black Monday.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:42.140
On October 19th, 1987, stock prices crashed
all over the world, without warning or explanation.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:47.490
According to the efficient market hypothesis,
stock prices are only supposed to change based

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:53.570
on new information, and yet there was little
to precipitate the crash other than that investors

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.580
all got “uneasy” at the same time.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:02.580
How, wondered Thaler, could a stock’s price
be 25% lower than it was the day before and

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.390
yet “both be rational measures of intrinsic
value, given the absence of news”?

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:11.870
This is not to say that the stock market is
pure fantasy.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:16.970
Quarterly reports and profit margins do matter,
and you can’t have a high share price for

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.490
long without something real to back it up.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:25.970
But the work of Thaler and other economists
did convince the financial community that

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:32.340
there was some element of human irrationality
at play and it could be a cause of market

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.340
volatility.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:35.250
So where does that leave us today?

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:39.780
No one knows for sure why the stock market
is doing well during an economic crisis, but

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.970
there are some theories.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:45.770
For one, the federal government has been pouring
billions of dollars into the economy--and

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:50.670
that kind of sudden liquidity can be like
a shot of adrenaline, even if the underlying

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.120
economy is weak.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.350
A second theory is that there’s nowhere
else for the money to go.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:08:01.930
Bonds currently have very low, even negative
returns, so investors have no choice but to

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:03.660
focus on stocks.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:09.690
And with sports and casinos closed, even gamblers
are turning to the stock market as an alternate

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:11.410
table to place their bets.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:13.350
And then there’s good old-fashioned FOMO.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:19.160
In a time of economic uncertainty, the stock
market seems to be the one thing that’s

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.270
going well, so no one wants to be left behind.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:27.040
Whatever the cause of the de-coupling, many
experts warn that it probably won’t last

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:28.040
forever.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:32.439
A new round of bad economic numbers, or the
Fed turning off the spigot might pull it back

00:08:32.439 --> 00:08:33.659
down to earth.

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:35.229
Does that mean you should get out?

00:08:35.229 --> 00:08:37.070
Not necessarily.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:42.219
People who hop in and out of the stock market
tend to lose money compared to people who

00:08:42.219 --> 00:08:44.670
just sit tight and wait it out.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:50.069
Richard Thaler’s best piece of advice for
investors is to rarely check your portfolio

00:08:50.069 --> 00:08:52.380
and avoid reading the news.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:58.110
The stock market is not an oracle with all
the answers, nor a casino where anything goes.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:04.589
It’s a tool for growing your finances, and
like most tools, it’s safest to handle when

00:09:04.589 --> 00:09:07.020
you’re calm 
and collected.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:08.600
And that's our two cents!

